1
00:00:00,270 --> 00:00:00,950
Look around your room.

2
00:00:01,070 --> 00:00:06,270
The walls around you, the light overhead, the air going in and out of your lungs without you ever thinking about it.

3
00:00:06,310 --> 00:00:09,710
All of it feels permanent, like the world has always been like this.

4
00:00:09,850 --> 00:00:10,450
It hasn't.

5
00:00:10,690 --> 00:00:15,550
Rewind far enough, and this exact spot becomes a place that would kill you in under a minute.

6
00:00:15,810 --> 00:00:17,090
There is no air you could breathe.

7
00:00:17,250 --> 00:00:21,730
The ground is literally liquid rock, and the sky glows orange like the inside of a furnace.

8
00:00:22,030 --> 00:00:26,330
For more than 99% of this planet's existence, nothing remotely like you was here.

9
00:00:26,530 --> 00:00:30,030
Earth got along just fine without us for four and a half billion years.

10
00:00:30,230 --> 00:00:35,750
Something was ruling the planet that entire time, and for almost all of it, it was nothing you'd recognize.

11
00:00:36,050 --> 00:00:39,770
Four and a half billion years is a number your brain simply can't hold.

12
00:00:39,930 --> 00:00:42,570
So forget the numbers for a second. Think of it like this.

13
00:00:42,730 --> 00:00:44,570
Imagine a stadium filled with popcorn.

14
00:00:44,690 --> 00:00:52,270
If all of that popcorn represented the entire history of Earth, then all of human history would be equal to one single kernel near the top of the pile.

15
00:00:52,430 --> 00:00:53,790
That is how small we actually are.

16
00:00:53,950 --> 00:00:59,190
We are a single grain of sand in the whole Sahara Desert, a single drop of water in the entire ocean.

17
00:00:59,410 --> 00:01:04,070
So what would happen if you stepped out onto Earth as it was four and a half billion years ago?

18
00:01:04,230 --> 00:01:07,430
Well, the first thing that happens is, you die. The entire surface is an ocean of lava under a thick orange haze of carbon dioxide.

19
00:01:12,290 --> 00:01:14,970
There's no moon in the sky either. It hasn't formed yet.

20
00:01:15,170 --> 00:01:21,090
A planet about the size of Mars, which scientists named Theia, has just slammed into the young Earth at an angle.

21
00:01:21,290 --> 00:01:30,150
The crash threw an enormous spray of debris into orbit, and over millions of years that debris will slowly clump together into the moon you see in the sky.

22
00:01:30,290 --> 00:01:34,250
The same impact also knocked the planet over by about 23 degrees.

23
00:01:34,570 --> 00:01:37,910
And that little tilt is the only reason we have seasons at all.

24
00:01:38,070 --> 00:01:41,690
Day one and a slightly different impact would have meant we never happened.

25
00:01:41,930 --> 00:01:45,310
Once the lava cooled and the first oceans formed, life appeared.

26
00:01:45,450 --> 00:01:47,950
But you would not have recognized it as life at all.

27
00:01:48,070 --> 00:01:52,310
For the next two billion years, this planet belonged to things too small to see.

28
00:01:52,510 --> 00:01:57,150
They were single-celled microbes, a whole living world that was invisible to the naked eye.

29
00:01:57,290 --> 00:02:03,370
There were no plants, no animals, nothing with a face at all, just specks of slime running the entire Earth.

30
00:02:03,490 --> 00:02:04,970
Do not mistake them for primitive.

31
00:02:05,290 --> 00:02:11,110
These specks ruled longer than every plant, every animal, and every dinosaur that ever lived combined.

32
00:02:11,330 --> 00:02:15,120
The air back then would have killed you. One breath of it with no oxygen at all, and you'd drop where you stood.

33
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:19,970
Then those microbes did something that almost ended everything. One breath of it with no oxygen at all, and you'd drop where you stood.

34
00:02:20,250 --> 00:02:21,750
Some of them learned to eat sunlight.

35
00:02:21,950 --> 00:02:23,270
We call it photosynthesis.

36
00:02:23,890 --> 00:02:26,550
Photosynthesis gives off a waste gas, oxygen.

37
00:02:26,810 --> 00:02:28,670
To you, oxygen is life.

38
00:02:28,970 --> 00:02:32,090
To almost everything alive back then, it was poison.

39
00:02:32,250 --> 00:02:36,390
As it built up in the air, it wiped out huge amounts of the life that couldn't handle it.

40
00:02:36,590 --> 00:02:43,810
Geologists call this the Great Oxidation Event, which is a very polite name for one of the biggest die-offs the planet has ever seen.

41
00:02:43,910 --> 00:02:45,630
But the same gas did something else.

42
00:02:45,830 --> 00:02:48,710
It stripped away the gases that had been trapping the planet's heat.

43
00:02:48,890 --> 00:02:54,690
And without them, the whole thing froze over, very possibly from pole to pole, into a single ball of ice.

44
00:02:54,870 --> 00:03:01,670
So the air sitting in your lungs right now is the leftover from a catastrophe, one that nearly wiped out the very life that created it.

45
00:03:01,770 --> 00:03:03,210
Life clung on under the ice.

46
00:03:03,430 --> 00:03:07,870
But for more than a billion years after that, the biggest thing alive was still microscopic.

47
00:03:08,370 --> 00:03:16,390
Then, a little over half a billion years ago, in a burst called the Cambrian Explosion, life suddenly got big, and weird, and fast.

48
00:03:16,610 --> 00:03:19,910
For the first time, the oceans filled with creatures you could actually see.

49
00:03:20,110 --> 00:03:23,230
Plants crept out onto bare rock and built the first soil.

50
00:03:23,430 --> 00:03:25,930
Then, fish in the shallows slowly grew legs.

51
00:03:26,170 --> 00:03:29,690
And the first uneasy animals hauled themselves up onto dry land.

52
00:03:29,990 --> 00:03:30,710
Forests rose.

53
00:03:31,010 --> 00:03:32,890
Swamps spread for thousands of miles.

54
00:03:33,130 --> 00:03:37,650
The coal we dig up and burn today is the buried remains of those exact forests.

55
00:03:37,950 --> 00:03:44,250
After four billion years of fire, ice, and poison, Earth was finally green, and warm, and alive.

56
00:03:44,390 --> 00:03:45,670
And then it tried to die again.

57
00:03:45,850 --> 00:03:48,170
This is where the world gets genuinely alien.

58
00:03:48,390 --> 00:03:53,790
Picture Earth around 260 million years ago, long before a single dinosaur exists.

59
00:03:53,970 --> 00:03:59,510
The days are shorter here, only about 22 hours long, because the planet is spinning faster than it does today.

60
00:03:59,730 --> 00:04:03,330
And the thing in charge isn't a dinosaur. Dinosaurs don't exist yet.

61
00:04:03,410 --> 00:04:06,090
It's something older, and arguably worse.

62
00:04:06,290 --> 00:04:11,470
They were called Gorgonopsids, and they were among the very first animals on Earth to grow saber-teeth.

63
00:04:11,610 --> 00:04:22,990
The biggest of them was a monster called Inostrinsevia, which is basically the scientific name for a bear-sized killing machine, with curved fangs as long as your hand, built to slice straight through flesh.

64
00:04:23,190 --> 00:04:27,370
For a long stretch of this world, if you were a plant-eater, it didn't matter how big you grew.

65
00:04:27,510 --> 00:04:28,970
One of these things was waiting for you.

66
00:04:29,190 --> 00:04:31,490
Strangely, the safest place to be was underwater.

67
00:04:31,690 --> 00:04:36,110
The seas back then were calm, and mostly free of anything big enough to hurt you.

68
00:04:36,290 --> 00:04:38,530
On land, there were no breaks at all.

69
00:04:38,730 --> 00:04:41,550
And then came the closest call life on Earth has ever had.

70
00:04:41,750 --> 00:04:53,350
Around 252 million years ago, a region of what's now Siberia tore open and stayed open, pouring out lava across an area the size of Western Europe, on and off for hundreds of thousands of years.

71
00:04:53,610 --> 00:04:57,330
It pumped so much carbon dioxide into the air that the whole planet roasted.

72
00:04:57,650 --> 00:05:01,870
The oceans heated toward bathwater temperatures, and the oxygen in the sea crashed.

73
00:05:02,030 --> 00:05:06,890
By the time it finally ended, the overwhelming majority of species on Earth were simply gone.

74
00:05:07,050 --> 00:05:09,670
By some estimates, as many as 9 in every 10.

75
00:05:09,790 --> 00:05:12,470
This is the event geologists call the Great Dying.

76
00:05:12,710 --> 00:05:14,970
Life did not recover in a few thousand years.

77
00:05:15,130 --> 00:05:19,470
It came within a hair of ending altogether, and it stayed broken for a very long time.

78
00:05:19,650 --> 00:05:22,950
Recovery, when it came, arrived in the strangest possible form.

79
00:05:23,370 --> 00:05:23,650
Rain.

80
00:05:24,010 --> 00:05:27,890
Starting around 234 million years ago, the skies opened.

81
00:05:28,110 --> 00:05:31,110
And then they simply kept going for about 2 million years.

82
00:05:31,370 --> 00:05:35,630
A world that had been hot and bone dry turned into a planet of constant storms.

83
00:05:35,790 --> 00:05:41,770
In that soggy, transformed landscape, a group of animals that had been minor background players finally got their shot.

84
00:05:41,910 --> 00:05:45,830
At the start of all that rain, dinosaurs were maybe 5% of the land animals.

85
00:05:46,010 --> 00:05:48,670
By the time it stopped, they were more than 90%.

86
00:05:48,670 --> 00:05:53,790
The rain didn't just refill the rivers, it handed the entire planet over to the dinosaurs.

87
00:05:54,030 --> 00:05:57,370
And they held onto it for more than 150 million years.

88
00:05:57,750 --> 00:05:59,850
You probably know roughly how their rain ends.

89
00:06:00,110 --> 00:06:06,730
66 million years ago, a rock around 10 kilometers wide slammed into the Earth and rewrote the rules in a single afternoon.

90
00:06:06,890 --> 00:06:11,930
The impact threw so much rock and ash into the sky that the sun effectively went out for months.

91
00:06:12,190 --> 00:06:13,370
Plants died in the dark.

92
00:06:13,590 --> 00:06:18,990
Everything that ate plants starved, and everything that ate those animals starved right behind them.

93
00:06:18,990 --> 00:06:20,710
The giants had no chance.

94
00:06:20,970 --> 00:06:29,510
But the small, furry, frightened little things that could burrow underground, waded out, and eat almost anything crawled through the wreckage and survived.

95
00:06:29,690 --> 00:06:32,110
Those small, furry survivors were mammals. Your ancestors.

96
00:06:33,990 --> 00:06:36,290
And here is the part actually worth sitting with.

97
00:06:36,370 --> 00:06:42,570
If that asteroid misses, the dinosaurs almost certainly keep running the show, and those little survivors stay small and forgettable forever.

98
00:06:42,770 --> 00:06:44,710
That rock didn't just end a world.

99
00:06:44,850 --> 00:06:46,730
It opened the only door we ever had.

100
00:06:46,890 --> 00:06:51,990
The single worst day in the history of large life on Earth is the entire reason you exist.

101
00:06:52,210 --> 00:06:54,630
Even then, we very nearly didn't make it.

102
00:06:54,750 --> 00:07:01,210
When our own species, Homo sapiens, finally appeared around 300,000 years ago, we were not the only humans alive.

103
00:07:01,510 --> 00:07:04,530
At least four other human species were here at the very same time.

104
00:07:04,630 --> 00:07:07,830
Other kinds of people, as real as we are, sharing the same world.

105
00:07:08,010 --> 00:07:10,530
Some of them had been around far longer than we had.

106
00:07:10,670 --> 00:07:15,590
Today, every single one of them is gone, and we are the only kind of human left standing.

107
00:07:15,710 --> 00:07:17,670
We are not the main characters of this story.

108
00:07:17,830 --> 00:07:20,650
We are the survivors of the survivors of the survivors.

109
00:07:21,030 --> 00:07:22,550
So look around the room one more time.

110
00:07:22,670 --> 00:07:25,530
Everything in it is balanced on top of a stack of disasters.

111
00:07:25,790 --> 00:07:30,990
A frozen planet, an extinction that almost wiped the board clean, and a rock from space that erased an entire world to make room for yours.

112
00:07:31,150 --> 00:07:39,150
Take away any single one of those, and the air never reaches your lungs, and this exact moment never happens.

113
00:07:39,330 --> 00:07:43,410
The world before us was never a warm -up act, waiting around for humans to show up.

114
00:07:43,550 --> 00:07:50,270
It was four and a half billion years of a planet that did not need us at all, running on lava, and microbes, and monsters, and luck.

115
00:07:50,450 --> 00:07:51,310
We are new here.

116
00:07:51,490 --> 00:07:52,710
Almost impossibly new.

117
00:07:52,910 --> 00:07:55,770
And the strangest part is that you were never the point of any of it.

118
00:07:55,890 --> 00:07:59,630
You are simply what happened to be left standing when the dust finally settled.

